Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 4 useful new finds

This pass screened 25+ plausible projects/results across GitHub,
Hugging Face/community trails, pet lip-sync, browser TTS, transparent
media, WebAR and commerce automation, and removed prior-run
discoveries.

-

HeadTTS =E2=80=94 met4citizen/HeadTTS

https://github.com/met4citizen/HeadTTS

MIT; 50 commits; 26 forks. Free Kokoro TTS that returns audio plus
word timestamps, phonemes and Oculus visemes (aa, E, I, O, U, PP, SS,
TH, CH, FF, kk, nn, RR, DD, sil). Runs entirely in browser via
WebGPU/WASM or as Node REST/WebSocket service. Uses MIT/Apache runtime
dependencies and explicitly avoids GPL eSpeak.

ROAST.PET STEP SOLVED: TTS + caption timing + mouth-state timing in
ONE inference. This can delete our proposed Kokoro =E2=86=92 Whisper
timestamps =E2=86=92 phoneme/viseme conversion chain.

ROUTE: npm/CDN @met4citizen/headtts; connect to webgpu/wasm or POST
/v1/synthesize. Output already contains words/wtimes/wdurations +
visemes/vtimes/vdurations + phonemes.

DEPENDENCIES/HW: Transformers.js + ONNX Kokoro; browser WebGPU
preferred, WASM fallback slower; CPU/WebGPU Node supported. English
only currently.

EVIDENCE: browser demo, REST/WebSocket implementation, tests, 50
commits, explicit timestamp/viseme response schema.

WORK REPLACED: likely the whole speech-timing/viseme worker and
separate transcription pass.

DIFFICULTY: LOW. MAINTENANCE: LOW-MEDIUM.

EXPERIMENT: generate 20 canonical Roast.pet roast lines and map its 15
Oculus visemes down to our 5-8 pet mouth sprites. Compare sync against
Kokoro=E2=86=92Whisper and amplitude-only Catsync.

VERDICT: REUSE NOW.

-

Alpha Video Kit =E2=80=94 stepancar/alpha-video-kit

https://github.com/stepancar/alpha-video-kit

MIT; 36 commits; 4 stars. Uses stacked-alpha video: RGB in top half,
grayscale alpha in bottom half, decoded by WebGL/WebGPU/SVG/Canvas.
Because the underlying file is ordinary opaque H.264/AV1/VP9, it
avoids native alpha-codec compatibility entirely. Provides custom
elements with a normal -like API and an in-browser ffmpeg.wasm
converter.

ROAST.PET STEP SOLVED: portable transparent animated pets without
maintaining WebM-alpha vs HEVC-alpha Safari branches.

ROUTE: npm @alpha-video-kit/webgl (or webgpu/svg); .

DEPENDENCIES/HW: normal browser video decode + WebGL/WebGPU; converter
uses ffmpeg.wasm.

EVIDENCE: three renderer backends, demo, browser tests, 36 commits, MIT.

WORK REPLACED: transparent codec detection/fallback code and
potentially dual asset generation.

DIFFICULTY: LOW. MAINTENANCE: LOW.

EXPERIMENT: encode the same 5-second transparent pet as (A) VP9 alpha
+ HEVC alpha and (B) stacked-alpha H.264; test iPhone Safari + Android
Chrome for bytes, decode CPU, edge quality and startup.

VERDICT: BENCHMARK NOW / likely REUSE.

-

arspace =E2=80=94 emb-ai/arspace

https://github.com/emb-ai/arspace

MIT; 52 commits; 16 stars. Browser WebAR project made to animate
printed research posters. Crucially, it is not merely another
one-target demo: media is organized as image/video pairs, a Python
manifest generator scans the directory automatically, optimization
scripts compress images/video, and multiple MindAR targets map to
media by manifest/index.

ROAST.PET STEP SOLVED: scalable card/poster =E2=86=92 AR-video packaging wh=
en
there are many customer assets, rather than hand-editing JS per order.

ROUTE: put card.jpg + card.mp4 into assets/media, optimize, compile
target(s), run tools/generate-manifest.py, static deploy.

DEPENDENCIES/HW: MindAR, browser camera, Python utilities, static HTTPS hos=
ting.

EVIDENCE: live arspace.ru demo, 52 commits, explicit multi-media
manifest workflow.

WORK REPLACED: order-specific AR JS editing, media manifest plumbing
and basic asset optimization scripts.

DIFFICULTY: LOW. MAINTENANCE: LOW.

EXPERIMENT: generate 10 fake Roast.pet card/video pairs, run manifest
generation, compile one multi-target .mind and verify every physical
card launches only its matching roast.

VERDICT: COPY/FORK PACKAGING MECHANISM.

-

HeadAudio =E2=80=94 met4citizen/HeadAudio

https://github.com/met4citizen/HeadAudio

MIT, dependency-free browser AudioWorklet. Uses MFCC feature vectors +
Gaussian prototypes + Mahalanobis-distance classification to generate
Oculus viseme blend-shape values in real time from arbitrary audio.
Tested in Chrome/Edge/Firefox/Safari desktop plus iPad/iPhone. Author
explicitly notes it is less accurate than text-driven lip sync.

ROAST.PET STEP SOLVED: lip-sync for customer-uploaded audio,
recordings, or arbitrary clips where we do NOT have TTS
text/timestamps.

ROUTE: browser AudioWorklet integrated into animation loop; consumes
audio, emits viseme weights.

DEPENDENCIES/HW: browser only; no server/external dependencies.

EVIDENCE: working tester/demo and cross-browser/mobile testing
reported by author.

WORK REPLACED: server transcription/phoneme alignment for the fallback
arbitrary-audio path.

DIFFICULTY: LOW-MEDIUM. MAINTENANCE: LOW.

EXPERIMENT: same 10 spoken clips through HeadAudio vs Catsync
amplitude mapping; collapse both to our pet mouth states and
blind-score perceived sync.

VERDICT: BENCHMARK / COPY FALLBACK.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

- TTS=E2=86=92word timestamps=E2=86=92phonemes=E2=86=92visemes: HeadTTS alr=
eady returns all of
them in one call.

- A separate Whisper pass for generated Roast.pet speech: unnecessary
if HeadTTS timing is accurate enough.

- Browser alpha-codec compatibility logic before testing stacked-alpha H.26=
4.

- Per-order hand-edited AR pages/manifests: arspace already turns
media folders into manifests.

- Transcript/phoneme infrastructure for arbitrary uploaded audio
before benchmarking HeadAudio as the cheap fallback.

ARCHITECTURE UPDATE

The speech path can become dramatically smaller:

script =E2=86=92 HeadTTS =E2=86=92 {audio, words/times, phonemes, visemes/t=
imes} =E2=86=92 map
15 human visemes to 5-8 pet mouth states =E2=86=92 deterministic actor =E2=
=86=92
transparent pet video =E2=86=92 Alpha Video Kit =E2=86=92 AR/card/social.

For non-TTS audio:

audio =E2=86=92 HeadAudio (better viseme hypothesis) OR Catsync (cheap
amplitude fallback) =E2=86=92 same pet-mouth-state interface.

MOST SURPRISING HOBBY PROJECT

HeadTTS. It already emits the exact three artifacts Roast.pet was
going to derive separately=E2=80=94audio, word-level caption timing, and
lip-sync visemes=E2=80=94from Kokoro, locally and MIT licensed.

FASTEST INTEGRATION WIN

HeadTTS. Generate one roast, read visemes/vtimes, collapse to our
mouth sprite set and render. This is a one-evening benchmark that
could delete multiple pipeline stages.

REPO/AUTHOR WORTH FOLLOWING

met4citizen. HeadTTS + HeadAudio together cover both halves of our
problem: exact text-driven lip sync when we control speech, and
real-time audio-driven visemes when we do not. Their TalkingHead
adjacency is also directly relevant to our actor-control architecture.
